<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yin Rugeng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Yin Ju-keng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yin Rugeng was a Chinese official, educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, who in November 1935 declared eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> autonomous from the Nationalist government and headed the Japanese-backed East Hebei Autonomous Government at Tongzhou. The regime ended after the Tongzhou mutiny of 1937 and was merged into a larger Japanese client government. After the war he was tried as a collaborator and executed in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yin-rugeng.jpg" alt="Yin Rugeng" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 山縣初男 (Hatsuo Yamagata) (?); scanned by 天竺鼠 14:49, 30 March 2013 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yin_Rugeng5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
